What information/access was obtained and Technical Interpretation:
Configuration Breach: The 230 Login successful response code confirmed that the vsFTPd 3.0.5 server permitted anonymous access, directly exposing infrastructure data.
Movement and Exfiltration: Upon executing the cd backup/acme-hyper-branding command, the server responded with a 250 Directory successfully changed status code, 
validating the existence of an exposed corporate backup directory. From this point, the process moved forward to identify and download (exfiltrate) 
critical files for subsequent static analysis—most notably, the source code of the main web application (app.py).


Phase 3: Source Code Review & Code Analysis (app.py)
What the technique does: It corresponds to a static code analysis phase (White-box Source Code Review) conducted after successfully exfiltrating 
the files from the FTP server. The objective is to audit the internal structure of the application to identify hidden routes, environment variables,
active debugging configurations, or logical flaws in request handling.

Why it works: Having direct access to the source code eliminates the need for blind testing (Black-box fuzzing). 
It allows for a microscopic inspection of how the application processes user data, mapping out all available endpoints (web routes),
and verifying the presence of sanitization failures or insecure execution modes.
