What information/access was obtained and Technical Interpretation:
Active Debug Mode: The line app.debug = True reveals that the Flask web server is running in development mode. In production environments,
this is extremely dangerous because if an unhandled exception occurs, Flask will render an interactive Werkzeug console in the browser. 
This potentially allows for Remote Code Execution (RCE) via default or leaked PINs.
Dangerous Module Imports: The presence of import subprocess indicates that the application directly interacts with the underlying operating system
at the shell level.
Route Exposure Surface: The endpoint @app.route('/files/<path:path>') was discovered. Although the snippet is truncated in the capture, 
the use of a dynamic parameter <path:path> combined with functions like send_from_directory or send_file indicates a high risk of Path Traversal 
(Directory Traversal) or arbitrary file read vulnerabilities if the input parameter is not rigorously sanitized.

Database Exploitation & Sensitive Data Exfiltration
What the technique does: This technique involves exploiting insecurely stored credentials discovered during source code/infrastructure reviews
to gain direct administrative access to a backend database server. Once inside, it utilizes database querying techniques to map out tables and exfiltrate
highly sensitive corporate records, such as cryptographic password hashes and user accounts

Why it works: Developers often leave hardcoded credentials within configuration files or container manifests (like a Dockerfile) in internal environments.
If the database service (in this case, MariaDB running on 10.1.0.16) is exposed across the internal subnet without strict network-level access controls, 
an attacker who uncovers these credentials can bypass all web-tier application logic and interact directly with the database engine.

