What information/access was obtained & Technical Interpretation:
Credential Leakage Exploitation: Analyzing the Dockerfile exposed the plaintext credentials (wordpress / wordpress).
This validated a critical security flaw: storing sensitive secrets inside image blueprints.

Direct Database Access: Using the native mysql-client, a remote connection was successfully established to the MariaDB instance.
The show tables; command confirmed the host runs a WordPress database schema, containing 12 distinct tables.

Critical Data Exfiltration: By targeting the wp_users table, corporate credentials for the administrator account jtorres were stolen (jtorres@acme-impact-alliance.com).

Cryptographic Analysis: The acquired password hash ($P$BVO7KQKhlmQ/M5f.cbQNyegYC38cNG/) uses the phpass / MD5 (WordPress) hashing algorithm. 
This hash can now be extracted into an external cracking system (like Hashcat or John the Ripper) to reverse it into plaintext, 
providing the exact password needed to hijack the user's sessions across other enterprise platforms.
